1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,567
One slow request.

2
00:00:01,567 --> 00:00:04,077
A backend takes a full minute to answer,

3
00:00:04,077 --> 00:00:05,458
and the visitor waits,

4
00:00:05,458 --> 00:00:08,031
holding a connection open the whole time.

5
00:00:08,581 --> 00:00:11,667
Now add a three megabyte upload nobody expected,

6
00:00:11,667 --> 00:00:14,110
a secret file nobody meant to publish,

7
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:17,903
and a server that announces its exact version to the world.

8
00:00:18,453 --> 00:00:20,016
Today you set the limits.

9
00:00:20,016 --> 00:00:21,516
How long engine X waits,

10
00:00:21,516 --> 00:00:22,766
how much it accepts,

11
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:24,704
and what it is willing to show.

12
00:00:25,254 --> 00:00:26,257
Set the limits.

13
00:00:27,807 --> 00:00:30,879
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

14
00:00:31,429 --> 00:00:34,970
One: the three kinds of limit, time, size, and exposure.

15
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:40,598
Two: a live demo. We find four risks in a real engine X, with real requests.

16
00:00:41,148 --> 00:00:43,307
Three: we fix them in the config.

17
00:00:43,307 --> 00:00:43,896
Timeouts,

18
00:00:43,896 --> 00:00:44,747
a size limit,

19
00:00:44,747 --> 00:00:45,859
a hidden version,

20
00:00:45,859 --> 00:00:46,972
and no dot files.

21
00:00:47,522 --> 00:00:50,991
Four: we repeat each request and watch the answer change.

22
00:00:50,991 --> 00:00:51,905
Then your turn,

23
00:00:51,905 --> 00:00:54,583
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

24
00:00:55,133 --> 00:00:57,834
This is lesson six of the web servers domain.

25
00:00:57,834 --> 00:00:59,515
Last time you read the logs.

26
00:00:59,515 --> 00:01:01,256
Today you protect the server.

27
00:01:01,806 --> 00:01:02,808
Set the limits.

28
00:01:04,858 --> 00:01:08,250
Three kinds of limit protect a web server. Let us meet them.

29
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:09,130
Time.

30
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:12,234
How long engine X waits: for the app behind it,

31
00:01:12,234 --> 00:01:14,678
for a slow visitor to send a request,

32
00:01:14,678 --> 00:01:17,056
and for an idle connection to close.

33
00:01:17,606 --> 00:01:17,941
Size.

34
00:01:17,941 --> 00:01:19,614
How big a request may be.

35
00:01:19,614 --> 00:01:21,488
The default is one megabyte,

36
00:01:21,488 --> 00:01:23,897
and a value of zero means unlimited,

37
00:01:23,897 --> 00:01:25,905
which is rarely what you want.

38
00:01:26,455 --> 00:01:27,010
Exposure.

39
00:01:27,010 --> 00:01:29,912
What the server gives away: its version number,

40
00:01:29,912 --> 00:01:31,703
and any file in the web root,

41
00:01:31,703 --> 00:01:34,049
including dot files that hold secrets.

42
00:01:34,599 --> 00:01:37,012
Each limit has its own status code.

43
00:01:37,012 --> 00:01:38,597
Too big: four thirteen.

44
00:01:38,597 --> 00:01:40,871
A backend too slow: five oh four.

45
00:01:40,871 --> 00:01:43,559
A file you may not have: four oh three.

46
00:01:45,109 --> 00:01:50,229
Go to the lab folder.

47
00:01:51,779 --> 00:01:53,678
Look around. Type: L S.

48
00:01:54,228 --> 00:01:54,944
The config,

49
00:01:54,944 --> 00:01:55,920
two test files,

50
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,613
a small one and a big one,

51
00:01:57,613 --> 00:01:58,785
and a site folder.

52
00:01:58,785 --> 00:01:59,827
Behind engine X,

53
00:01:59,827 --> 00:02:01,780
a slow app is already running.

54
00:02:02,330 --> 00:02:12,335
First risk: the version.

55
00:02:12,885 --> 00:02:13,972
The exact version.

56
00:02:13,972 --> 00:02:16,751
An attacker now knows which known bugs to try.

57
00:02:16,751 --> 00:02:18,624
There is no reason to share it.

58
00:02:19,174 --> 00:02:27,344
Second risk: a secret file.

59
00:02:27,894 --> 00:02:29,139
A database password,

60
00:02:29,139 --> 00:02:30,197
served to anyone.

61
00:02:30,197 --> 00:02:33,558
The web root is public: anything in it can be fetched,

62
00:02:33,558 --> 00:02:34,678
unless you say no.

63
00:02:35,228 --> 00:02:43,186
Third risk: size.

64
00:02:43,736 --> 00:02:45,213
Two hundred: accepted.

65
00:02:45,213 --> 00:02:47,696
In the config the size limit is zero,

66
00:02:47,696 --> 00:02:48,368
unlimited.

67
00:02:48,368 --> 00:02:51,053
One careless client could fill the disk.

68
00:02:51,603 --> 00:02:58,046
Fourth risk: time.

69
00:02:58,596 --> 00:02:59,341
Two hundred,

70
00:02:59,341 --> 00:03:00,522
after four seconds.

71
00:03:00,522 --> 00:03:01,516
Engine X waited,

72
00:03:01,516 --> 00:03:02,946
holding the connection.

73
00:03:02,946 --> 00:03:04,002
With the default,

74
00:03:04,002 --> 00:03:05,742
it would wait a full minute.

75
00:03:06,292 --> 00:03:11,348
Open the config in vim.

76
00:03:11,898 --> 00:03:13,195
Go to line fifteen,

77
00:03:13,195 --> 00:03:14,219
the size limit,

78
00:03:14,219 --> 00:03:15,790
and change it to one M.

79
00:03:15,790 --> 00:03:16,404
Under it,

80
00:03:16,404 --> 00:03:17,496
add the version,

81
00:03:17,496 --> 00:03:19,135
the two client timeouts,

82
00:03:19,135 --> 00:03:19,886
keep alive,

83
00:03:19,886 --> 00:03:22,411
and a location that denies dot files.

84
00:03:22,411 --> 00:03:22,753
Then,

85
00:03:22,753 --> 00:03:24,186
in the slow location,

86
00:03:24,186 --> 00:03:25,756
add proxy read timeout,

87
00:03:25,756 --> 00:03:26,576
two seconds.

88
00:03:55,926 --> 00:04:02,027
Test, then reload.

89
00:04:02,577 --> 00:04:04,305
Applied without stopping anything.

90
00:04:04,855 --> 00:04:12,130
Check the version again.

91
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:15,090
Just the word engine X. No version.

92
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:23,192
Ask for the secret again.

93
00:04:23,742 --> 00:04:27,156
Four oh three, forbidden. The password stays on the server.

94
00:04:27,706 --> 00:04:34,255
Send the big upload again.

95
00:04:34,805 --> 00:04:39,626
Four thirteen: request entity too large. Refused before the app ever saw it.

96
00:04:40,176 --> 00:04:48,027
Send the small upload.

97
00:04:48,577 --> 00:04:53,036
Still two hundred. A good limit stops the abuse and lets normal work through.

98
00:04:53,586 --> 00:04:58,962
Ask for the slow path again.

99
00:04:59,512 --> 00:05:01,524
Five oh four after two seconds.

100
00:05:01,524 --> 00:05:03,667
Engine X gave up on the slow app,

101
00:05:03,667 --> 00:05:05,290
and freed the connection.

102
00:05:05,290 --> 00:05:06,978
That is a gateway timeout.

103
00:05:07,528 --> 00:05:09,896
Run the judge.

104
00:05:10,446 --> 00:05:11,275
Five checks.

105
00:05:11,275 --> 00:05:12,933
The slow path times out.

106
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:16,386
The big upload is refused and the small one works.

107
00:05:16,386 --> 00:05:17,906
The version is hidden.

108
00:05:17,906 --> 00:05:19,425
Dot files are refused.

109
00:05:19,425 --> 00:05:21,774
And the client timeouts are short.

110
00:05:21,774 --> 00:05:22,948
Five out of five.

111
00:05:23,498 --> 00:05:25,460
Clean up.

112
00:05:26,010 --> 00:05:29,829
Engine X and the slow app are stopped, and everything is removed.

113
00:05:30,379 --> 00:05:37,227
Are the ports free? Count the lines.

114
00:05:37,777 --> 00:05:40,785
Zero. Nothing listens, nothing is left behind.

115
00:05:42,335 --> 00:05:47,604
Your task. Find four risks, fix them in the config, and prove each fix with a request.

116
00:05:48,154 --> 00:05:51,546
One: lab up, then send the four requests, and read each answer.

117
00:05:52,096 --> 00:05:55,851
Two: in vim, set the body size to one M, and hide the version.

118
00:05:56,401 --> 00:05:57,809
Three: deny dot files,

119
00:05:57,809 --> 00:06:02,289
and set the client timeouts and keep alive to fifteen seconds or less.

120
00:06:02,839 --> 00:06:08,172
Four: add proxy read timeout, two seconds, to the slow location. Test, and reload.

121
00:06:08,722 --> 00:06:10,350
Five: repeat the requests,

122
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:12,729
check that a small upload still works,

123
00:06:12,729 --> 00:06:13,606
run lab check,

124
00:06:13,606 --> 00:06:14,482
then lab down.

125
00:06:16,532 --> 00:06:20,308
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

126
00:06:20,858 --> 00:06:22,506
The lab lives on GitHub,

127
00:06:22,506 --> 00:06:23,673
at it dash waves,

128
00:06:23,673 --> 00:06:25,253
slash dev ops dash A I,

129
00:06:25,253 --> 00:06:26,763
in the folder academy,

130
00:06:26,763 --> 00:06:27,450
slash lab.

131
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,568
Two files: lab one twenty-three dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-three dot S H.

132
00:06:34,118 --> 00:06:35,002
Install it,

133
00:06:35,002 --> 00:06:37,814
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

134
00:06:37,814 --> 00:06:39,662
dash m seven five five,

135
00:06:39,662 --> 00:06:41,992
lab one twenty-three dot S H,

136
00:06:41,992 --> 00:06:43,198
into slash usr,

137
00:06:43,198 --> 00:06:44,162
slash local,

138
00:06:44,162 --> 00:06:45,126
slash S bin.

139
00:06:45,676 --> 00:06:50,483
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-three dot S H.

140
00:06:50,483 --> 00:06:51,668
You get two: lab,

141
00:06:51,668 --> 00:06:52,225
and N G.

142
00:06:52,775 --> 00:06:53,948
You need engine X,

143
00:06:53,948 --> 00:06:56,031
curl and python three installed,

144
00:06:56,031 --> 00:07:00,199
and ports eighty-two thirty-one and eighty-two thirty-nine free.

145
00:07:00,199 --> 00:07:02,674
The lab runs its own private engine X.

146
00:07:03,224 --> 00:07:04,931
Lab up prepares everything.

147
00:07:04,931 --> 00:07:06,638
Lab check judges your work.

148
00:07:06,638 --> 00:07:08,155
Lab down removes it all.

149
00:07:08,155 --> 00:07:10,179
Only run it on your own machine.

150
00:07:12,229 --> 00:07:14,298
Pass or fail. You need all five.

151
00:07:14,848 --> 00:07:17,195
The slow path gives up with a five oh four.

152
00:07:17,745 --> 00:07:20,710
A big upload is refused, a small one still works.

153
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:22,860
The server version is hidden.

154
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:26,290
Dot files are refused, normal pages work.

155
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:30,275
Client timeouts and keep alive are fifteen seconds or less.

156
00:07:30,825 --> 00:07:32,126
To pass, all five.

157
00:07:34,176 --> 00:07:35,947
Your turn, on your own machine.

158
00:07:36,497 --> 00:07:39,867
One: lab up, and send the four requests. Read the answers.

159
00:07:40,417 --> 00:07:44,983
Two: in vim, fix the size limit, hide the version, and deny dot files.

160
00:07:45,533 --> 00:07:48,509
Three: set the client timeouts and keep alive.

161
00:07:48,509 --> 00:07:51,357
Add proxy read timeout to the slow location.

162
00:07:51,907 --> 00:07:54,787
Four: test, reload, and repeat every request.

163
00:07:55,337 --> 00:07:59,987
Five: send the small upload, and run lab check until you see five out of five.

164
00:08:00,537 --> 00:08:03,375
Six: lab down, and prove both ports are free.

165
00:08:03,925 --> 00:08:05,759
Pause the video, and begin now.

166
00:08:21,309 --> 00:08:22,184
Quick check.

167
00:08:22,734 --> 00:08:24,334
What are the three kinds of limit?

168
00:08:24,884 --> 00:08:29,918
Which status code means a request was too big, and which means the backend was too slow?

169
00:08:30,468 --> 00:08:32,666
Why is a size limit of zero risky?

170
00:08:33,216 --> 00:08:36,074
Why hide the version, and why deny dot files?

171
00:08:36,624 --> 00:08:39,088
Objectives covered: M C thirteen,

172
00:08:39,088 --> 00:08:40,283
objective seven.

173
00:08:40,283 --> 00:08:40,955
Timeouts,

174
00:08:40,955 --> 00:08:43,942
request size limits and secure defaults.

175
00:08:44,492 --> 00:08:46,561
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

